WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.167 --> 00:00:02.235
-Our first guest tonight
is an actress,

00:00:02.235 --> 00:00:04.103
a Grammy Award-winning musician,

00:00:04.103 --> 00:00:06.606
and a member of
the Country Music Hall of Fame.

00:00:06.606 --> 00:00:09.275
Her new album and book,
both titled

00:00:09.275 --> 00:00:11.243
"Not That Fancy,"
are available now.

00:00:11.243 --> 00:00:12.645
She's also a coach
on "The Voice,"

00:00:12.645 --> 00:00:14.547
which airs Mondays
and Tuesdays here on NBC.

00:00:14.547 --> 00:00:15.414
Let's take a look.

00:00:15.414 --> 00:00:16.916
-I want some tots before we go.

00:00:16.916 --> 00:00:19.118
-Yeah, let's do it.
-I want some tots.

00:00:19.118 --> 00:00:21.654
-I am retiring
Reba's Tot Cart.

00:00:21.654 --> 00:00:23.790
-Reba's Team is full, y'all.

00:00:25.759 --> 00:00:27.760
-This is my first season
on "The Voice."

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:30.330
I've had a blast
filling my team

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:33.499
and I can't believe
how quickly it passed by.

00:00:33.499 --> 00:00:35.335
And that's what happens
when you're having fun.

00:00:35.335 --> 00:00:37.470
I'll just be over here
with my tater tots.

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:40.607
-[ Laughs ]
-Y'all need me.

00:00:40.607 --> 00:00:41.841
-Please welcome back to the show

00:00:41.841 --> 00:00:43.976
the one, the only
Reba McIntyre, everybody.

00:00:43.976 --> 00:00:45.278
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:45.278 --> 00:01:03.596 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:03.596 --> 00:01:04.697
Welcome back, Reba.

00:01:04.697 --> 00:01:06.465
What an honor to see you again.

00:01:06.465 --> 00:01:09.335
-So long since I've seen you.
-Well, it was last night.

00:01:09.335 --> 00:01:11.071
-Oh, was it? That's right.
-It was last night.

00:01:11.071 --> 00:01:12.471
-Time flies when you're
having fun.

00:01:12.471 --> 00:01:13.939
-We did a wonderful event at
town hall

00:01:13.939 --> 00:01:14.874
for your book last night

00:01:14.874 --> 00:01:16.409
right here in New York City.

00:01:16.409 --> 00:01:19.312
And my goodness, your fans
were out in full force.

00:01:19.312 --> 00:01:21.147
The people that love you,
they love you very much.

00:01:21.147 --> 00:01:22.448
And I hope you felt
it last night

00:01:22.448 --> 00:01:23.749
because that was
a special evening.

00:01:23.749 --> 00:01:24.984
-I thought it was a lot of fun.

00:01:24.984 --> 00:01:26.786
You did a great job.

00:01:26.786 --> 00:01:28.387
-Thank you.
I'm very glad I did it

00:01:28.387 --> 00:01:29.922
because now I'm going
to make you tell another story

00:01:29.922 --> 00:01:31.724
you told last night because
it was so good.

00:01:31.724 --> 00:01:34.626
You know, this book
is a very wonderful collection

00:01:34.626 --> 00:01:35.962
of stories and anecdotes,

00:01:35.962 --> 00:01:38.064
and you can tell how much
your family means to you,

00:01:38.064 --> 00:01:40.500
generations of the McIntyres.

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:41.901
But you were talking
about your mama.

00:01:41.901 --> 00:01:44.136
And I think it was a story about
your grandmother needed a drink.

00:01:44.136 --> 00:01:45.138
Is that right?
-No, Mama.

00:01:45.138 --> 00:01:46.272
-It was your mama. Okay.
-Yeah.

00:01:46.272 --> 00:01:47.940
We were at her house

00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:50.776
and all the family was there
and the grandkids and kids

00:01:50.776 --> 00:01:52.545
and everybody
was running around.

00:01:52.545 --> 00:01:55.348
And Mama's nerves had just
gotten about right to here.

00:01:55.348 --> 00:01:57.650
And I looked over
at her and I said, "Mama,

00:01:57.650 --> 00:01:59.018
you need a shot of who hit him?"

00:01:59.018 --> 00:02:00.620
[ Laughter ]

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:03.055
It's either a shot of who hit
him or a shot of who done it.

00:02:03.055 --> 00:02:04.657
-Okay.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:04.657 --> 00:02:06.892
-And she said, "Yeah,"
She told Lou Casey,

00:02:06.892 --> 00:02:08.995
her granddaughter,
she said, "Lou, come here."

00:02:08.995 --> 00:02:10.996
She said, "I want you
to go in my bedroom.

00:02:10.996 --> 00:02:12.198
Go in the bathroom,

00:02:12.198 --> 00:02:13.967
look in the bottom
of my dirty clothes hamper

00:02:13.967 --> 00:02:15.635
and bring me a bottle
of Crown out here."

00:02:15.635 --> 00:02:16.936
[ Laughter ]

00:02:16.936 --> 00:02:18.605
She said, "Got it."
And I said, "Mama,

00:02:18.605 --> 00:02:20.373
why do you put your Crown
in the bottom

00:02:20.373 --> 00:02:22.474
of your dirty clothes hamper?"
She said,

00:02:22.474 --> 00:02:24.610
"Do you think my grandsons
are going to go in there

00:02:24.610 --> 00:02:26.713
checking for Crown
in my dirty clothes?"

00:02:26.713 --> 00:02:29.549
[ Laughter ]

00:02:29.549 --> 00:02:31.250
-I love it.
She's one step ahead of them.

00:02:31.250 --> 00:02:32.351
-Telling you.
-She stayed one step

00:02:32.351 --> 00:02:35.988
ahead of them.
-Absolutely. Always.

00:02:35.988 --> 00:02:38.324
-Well, we were talking about
"The Voice" last night as well.

00:02:38.324 --> 00:02:41.193
I mean, obviously,
there's a great camaraderie

00:02:41.193 --> 00:02:42.561
between you
and the other judges.

00:02:42.561 --> 00:02:44.763
But is there one of them
you have your eye on

00:02:44.763 --> 00:02:46.933
as the toughest competition?
-John.

00:02:46.933 --> 00:02:48.801
-Yeah.
-John Legend. Yeah.

00:02:48.801 --> 00:02:51.937
John's got a great knack
for picking great singers.

00:02:51.937 --> 00:02:54.841
And when we're
with our backs

00:02:54.841 --> 00:02:57.710
to the singers
that come on to audition,

00:02:57.710 --> 00:02:59.445
I'm always kind of
going thataway,

00:02:59.445 --> 00:03:02.148
looking at John, seeing if
John's going to hit his button.

00:03:02.148 --> 00:03:05.585
Well, John's really sneaky.
He won't do anything.

00:03:05.585 --> 00:03:07.953
He'll just be [Humming]

00:03:07.953 --> 00:03:10.890
And go like that.
And we're all like, "Oh, man!"

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:12.258
-Yeah.
-But it's fun.

00:03:12.258 --> 00:03:14.060
-You seem like
you're having a fun time.

00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:15.294
-I really am.
-It's lovely.

00:03:15.294 --> 00:03:18.664
You also -- I hope you had fun
writing this book

00:03:18.664 --> 00:03:21.233
because like I said, it is
a collection of a lot of things.

00:03:21.233 --> 00:03:25.604
It's recipes, it's self-help,
it's your own story.

00:03:25.604 --> 00:03:28.808
And while you're writing it
and also the album as well,

00:03:28.808 --> 00:03:32.044
this is a collection of songs
you've done before, some new,

00:03:32.044 --> 00:03:34.580
but mostly just a reimagining
of some wonderful songs.

00:03:34.580 --> 00:03:38.984
And both of them are a real gift
to your, you know, your fans

00:03:38.984 --> 00:03:41.153
and people who maybe are going
to be a fan after they read it.

00:03:41.153 --> 00:03:44.390
But of these recipes,
can you just tell us real quick,

00:03:44.390 --> 00:03:46.893
your tater tots recipe,
because that is one that

00:03:46.893 --> 00:03:48.661
I'm the most confident
anyone can do.

00:03:48.661 --> 00:03:50.629
-Thank you very much.
Yes. You get in your car.

00:03:50.629 --> 00:03:51.930
-Yep.
-And you put it in reverse.

00:03:51.930 --> 00:03:53.966
Get out of the garage
and drive down the road

00:03:53.966 --> 00:03:58.838
and you find your nearest Sonic.
-Yep.

00:03:58.838 --> 00:04:00.472
-Pull in the entrance,
not the exit.

00:04:00.472 --> 00:04:01.908
-Yep.
-And you pull in one of

00:04:01.908 --> 00:04:04.744
them little slots and you push
that red button and you say,

00:04:04.744 --> 00:04:06.211
"Can I have me some tater tots?"

00:04:06.211 --> 00:04:08.113
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:04:08.113 --> 00:04:10.082
And I hope they don't say
"We're out."

00:04:10.082 --> 00:04:11.584
-Yeah. And you hope
you don't say they're out.

00:04:11.584 --> 00:04:13.252
Thank you for putting put
in the go in the entrance,

00:04:13.252 --> 00:04:15.354
not the exit, because I think
that's the one part

00:04:15.354 --> 00:04:16.956
I always get wrong
about the recipe.

00:04:16.956 --> 00:04:19.058
-Well, yeah.
Yeah, I've done that.

00:04:19.058 --> 00:04:21.794
-You do have this wonderful
new restaurant

00:04:21.794 --> 00:04:24.296
called Reba's Place in Atoka.
Am I saying that?

00:04:24.296 --> 00:04:25.497
Atoka, Oklahoma?
-Yes.

00:04:25.497 --> 00:04:28.567
-And that's also a music venue.

00:04:28.567 --> 00:04:29.969
-Yeah, it's got
a little bitty stage.

00:04:29.969 --> 00:04:31.737
-A little bitty stage.
But you were talking about

00:04:31.737 --> 00:04:33.506
how you kind of go in there
every now and then,

00:04:33.506 --> 00:04:35.575
and I would imagine it's called
Reba's Place, you're Reba.

00:04:35.575 --> 00:04:37.877
You walk in,
people get excited right away.

00:04:37.877 --> 00:04:39.478
But you were saying sometimes

00:04:39.478 --> 00:04:41.247
they just don't even
notice it's you.

00:04:41.247 --> 00:04:44.283
-Yeah, we did a surprise
appearance,

00:04:44.283 --> 00:04:47.252
and Pake, my older brother,
was up playing,

00:04:47.252 --> 00:04:50.756
and he kind of got down
and my band snuck up there,

00:04:50.756 --> 00:04:54.126
three or four of them,
and I came through the kitchen

00:04:54.126 --> 00:04:56.796
and hopped up on the bandstand
and started singing

00:04:56.796 --> 00:05:00.333
"Can't Even Get the Blues"
and nobody said anything.

00:05:00.333 --> 00:05:01.801
[ Laughter ]

00:05:01.801 --> 00:05:05.538
I mean, they didn't even look up
from their chicken fried steak.

00:05:05.538 --> 00:05:07.039
They were serious about it.

00:05:07.039 --> 00:05:08.641
And all of a sudden I went
[Clears throat]

00:05:08.641 --> 00:05:11.277
and they went, "What is that?"
And they go...

00:05:11.277 --> 00:05:13.011
-That's pretty exciting.
-Yeah, well.

00:05:13.011 --> 00:05:14.313
-I guess they just
couldn't believe Reba would

00:05:14.313 --> 00:05:16.015
actually be at Reba's Place.
But there you go.

00:05:16.015 --> 00:05:17.283
-I guess they thought it was,

00:05:17.283 --> 00:05:19.018
you know, playing over
the speakers or something.

00:05:19.018 --> 00:05:20.853
-It does speak
to the quality of the food.

00:05:20.853 --> 00:05:23.055
They won't even look up
from their chicken fried steak.

00:05:23.055 --> 00:05:24.723
-I'm going to use that.
-There you go.

00:05:24.723 --> 00:05:26.826
-Thank you.
-You have some farmers

00:05:26.826 --> 00:05:28.795
that are showing their love
for you

00:05:28.795 --> 00:05:31.797
in the launch of this book
and this album.

00:05:31.797 --> 00:05:34.600
I mean, I don't even know
how you go about this,

00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:36.436
but I will say
it's incredibly impressive.

00:05:36.436 --> 00:05:37.469
These are -- I guess they're

00:05:37.469 --> 00:05:40.172
just using some real good
lawnmowers.

00:05:40.172 --> 00:05:43.242
So there's Von Thun Farms.

00:05:43.242 --> 00:05:46.379
-I'm asked,
"How do they do that?"

00:05:46.379 --> 00:05:50.115
-Here's another one.
That's a very good Reba as well.

00:05:50.115 --> 00:05:52.652
I mean...
-How about that?

00:05:52.652 --> 00:05:55.154
-I mean, it's worth
getting a small plane

00:05:55.154 --> 00:05:56.689
just to go up and fly
around this country

00:05:56.689 --> 00:05:59.124
to see where your face
is turning up, right?

00:05:59.124 --> 00:06:02.761
-Do they put a computer chip
in the tractor or something?

00:06:02.761 --> 00:06:05.130
How do they do that?
-I think they might.

00:06:05.130 --> 00:06:06.632
I was trying to figure it out,
but you're right.

00:06:06.632 --> 00:06:08.935
There's probably computers.
-No idea. Yeah.

00:06:08.935 --> 00:06:10.335
-Every time you see something,
you're like,

00:06:10.335 --> 00:06:12.104
"How did they do that?"
The answer It's a computer.

00:06:12.104 --> 00:06:14.373
Yeah.
-It's not just they found a guy

00:06:14.373 --> 00:06:15.842
who really knows
what you look like

00:06:15.842 --> 00:06:18.544
and put him behind a tractor,
and you're like, "Do your best."

00:06:18.544 --> 00:06:20.879
-Yeah. "Go for it."

00:06:20.879 --> 00:06:23.916
-Do you have fun
rerecording old songs?

00:06:23.916 --> 00:06:25.985
-I do. Dave Cobb --

00:06:25.985 --> 00:06:30.456
He and I worked on this album
to strip it down

00:06:30.456 --> 00:06:33.291
and get just to the meat
of the song

00:06:33.291 --> 00:06:35.027
and not a big production,

00:06:35.027 --> 00:06:37.796
which I had done originally
for these songs.

00:06:37.796 --> 00:06:40.867
And so it just really
brought out the --

00:06:40.867 --> 00:06:42.868
the heart and soul of the song,
I think.

00:06:42.868 --> 00:06:44.136
Like, for instance,

00:06:44.136 --> 00:06:45.972
"Consider Me Gone"
is one of the songs on it.

00:06:45.972 --> 00:06:47.607
That's Rex's favorite song.

00:06:47.607 --> 00:06:51.110
And it just -- I like
the song more now

00:06:51.110 --> 00:06:53.078
when I get to sing it
because it's softer.

00:06:53.078 --> 00:06:54.780
-Well, that's really wonderful.

00:06:54.780 --> 00:06:56.715
You know, I got to see you
do a few songs last night.

00:06:56.715 --> 00:06:59.151
I can't wait to listen to
the whole album start to finish,

00:06:59.151 --> 00:07:00.386
and it's such a joy
to have you here.

00:07:00.386 --> 00:07:01.353
Thanks so much
for stopping by.

00:07:01.353 --> 00:07:02.588
-My pleasure.

00:07:02.588 --> 00:07:04.456
-That's Reba McIntyre,
everybody.

00:07:04.456 --> 00:07:05.758
"Not That Fancy,"

00:07:05.758 --> 00:07:08.460
the book and the album
are on sale now,

00:07:08.460 --> 00:07:10.062
and "The Voice" airs Mondays
and Tuesdays

00:07:10.062 --> 00:07:12.865
right here on NBC. We'll be
right back with Werner Herzog.

